you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  better late than never the much-delayed  environment bill which sets  binding targets to tackle climate change  is included in the queen's speech  we find out why six young people from  portugal are taking more than 30  countries to court  over climate change and growing  solutions how reforestation efforts are  paying off  around the world  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and we aim to take you to the  heart of the climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come now the queen's speech today set  out the government's legislative agenda  for the year ahead and there was a clear  focus on climate issues  it included the long-delayed environment  bill which will set  legally binding environmental targets  and commitments  to restoring nature and biodiversity  the lifetime skills guarantee is  expected to give better access to  education and training  something welcomed by green business  groups who warned the transition to net  zero  could otherwise be hampered by skill  shortages in the future  but proposals for the planning system  could prove controversial if fast-track  developments don't fully consider  environmental impacts or promote green  building practices  well our science correspondent thomas  moore joins me now welcome to you thomas  and there's been a long wait for the  environment bill hasn't there but what's  in it  well it's the third queen speech can you  believe that has appeared in  work started back in july 2018 but it  has been so badly delayed  held over from the last parliamentary  session because the government said  there wasn't enough time to debate it  properly during the covid crisis  well what is it well it is the biggest  shakeup to green regulation in several  decades it sets out the environmental  protections  for the uk now that eu rules no longer  apply  the government says that it will improve  air pollution water quality  waste management the climate and  biodiversity  but green groups are concerned they  believe that it doesn't set  tough enough targets they're too  long-term to drive the immediate action  that is needed during this nature crisis  they also feel that the watchdog the  office for environmental protection in  the bill  lacks both independence and teeth now  all this is happening  during the year of the big climate  summit in glasgow of course when the  world's eyes are on the uk  and green groups fear that uh the  government will fail to take  enough environmental leadership in such  an important year  and that will be hugely embarrassing for  the country for it to stand the chance  of being passed in parliament  in time work needs to start next month  it is that urgent thomas thank you  let's take a look now at some of the  day's other climate news and the  government has announced 12 million  pounds in new funding to support  developing countries to better prepare  for and  respond to climate-related disasters  the fund will help deal with the effects  of extreme weather such as droughts and  floods  as well as predicting climate events  such as heat waves  [Applause]  ministerial departments are being urged  to use sustainable  laptops to reduce their carbon footprint  circular computing estimates emissions  would be cut by more than 196  000 tonnes over the next 14 years  if refurbished laptops were used instead  of new ones  the sustainable it firm says it would  also save the government almost 128  million pounds  [Applause]  trees are being used as pollution  barriers to protect people  exercising in london drinks company  tenzing has begun planting trees in  highly polluted areas of the city  popular amongst runners and cyclists the  areas have been identified using  tensing's clean air tracker and  pollution data  from imperial college london  [Applause]  most people in britain want single-use  coffee cup lids  banned immediately to reduce plastic  waste  a new survey has found 73 of adults  would support a ban on plastic lids  and nearly two-thirds say they'd pay  more for an eco-friendly alternative  two and a half billion takeaway coffee  cups are thrown away each year and only  one in  400 of those are recycled  and an artist has planted a ghost forest  in new york to raise awareness about  deforestation  myelin has created the art installation  using  49 dead cedars that were originally  grown in the pine barrens  an ecosystem on the atlantic coast maya  hopes the exhibit will show people the  devastating impact  climate change has on the natural world  now last year saw a record surge in  global renewable power according to the  international energy agency  it found the renewables industry grew at  its fastest pace since 1999  with the delivery of renewable energy  projects increasing by 45 percent  now by far the biggest increases were in  solar capacity  and wind turbines and you can see here  on our data dashboard  the real-time power mix in great britain  and we can see that 43  of electricity today is coming from  fossil fuels  we've got 38 there from renewable  sources  and 19 coming from nuclear and  that's actually a marked difference from  yesterday when we saw  60 percent of electricity coming from  renewable sources  now who is responsible for climate  change it's hard to pin down  individuals governments and businesses  all play a part in causing global  warming  and what can be done about it in the  future but for six young people  they think that countries aren't doing  enough to fight the climate crisis  and are taking them to court over it  following the 2017  wildfires in portugal the group took  their case of the european court of  human rights  arguing not enough is being done to keep  global temperatures below  1.5 degrees and now the charity saved  the children  has intervened in the case well here to  explain more is enrique scillias  director of policy and advocacy at  save the children welcome to you so why  you say the children  intervening in this case what do you  hope to achieve thanks anna  we are intervening mainly for two  reasons children  are by far the hardest hit by the  climate crisis they are  they are hit in so many ways on their  health on their education  on what they can eat and whatnot and  it's not just about  the children today but it's also that  the future of the generations to come  and we want to make sure that when the  court are ruling on this case they have  as much information as possible  on how the climate crisis are hitting  children  and their rights so that they can take  as an informed decision as possible  secondly and also importantly we are  really really inspired to see  children like the portuguese children  taking action  and and and to be part of decisions and  formulating how the climate crisis  should be  be taken forward so just the fact that  these children are there and they are  taking action with the court is  something that we think are worth  supporting and making sure that children  can be part of these discussions and and  and formulating decisions  many countries would argue we are  setting targets to reduce our emissions  we're laying out  our policies so what would your  counter-argument to them be  countries need to be more and do more  and they need to listen to children and  take action on what children  are saying they need in order to  mitigate the impacts  of the climate crisis so we hope that  by by by intervening in this case and  supporting this case  we have we have enough the court has an  opportunity to put a spotlight  on the impact of the climate crisis on  children  and their rights and hopefully also to  to to push government to accelerate  their their action in this area  and do you think litigation is the best  way to do that how effective  is the legal process enforcing  government policy change  of course litigation is just one part of  the solution right and and  one part of what we can do to push for  more action in this area  but we know from around the world that  litigation  can be a very effective way of putting  again as i said before  putting spotlight on the issues that  need to be addressed  and to push governments to do more and  to do better and it is crucial  that at this time and leading up to  cop26  in glasgow that we really make sure that  governments are there  looking at what needs to happen in order  for children and their futures to be  protected  ulrika sileus thanks very much indeed  now we often hear about deforestation  and the devastating  impact it has on our planet and while  huge areas of forest  are disappearing there is hope nearly  60 million hectares of natural forests  have grown  back since 2000. that's around 22  billion  trees and they have the potential to  store the equivalent  of 5.9 gigatons of  co2 more than the annual emissions of  the whole of the united states  and it's all due to different types of  reforestation happening  around the world well let's bring in  will baldwin cantelo who's director of  nature-based solutions at wwf welcome to  you  so i'm reading that you found that an  area the size of france has regrown  naturally in the last 20 years that  sounds pretty encouraging  it is encouraging and it's great news  it's good to demonstrate  the power of boris to be able to regrow  given the  the space to do so and and by doing this  we're providing  a great solution in the fight against  climate change  and what we need to do though now is is  to  to make sure that we can secure these  forests in the long term we can't take  them for granted and of course  deforestation is continuing at pace  around these forests  so it's important to secure these  forests for the long term  well yes and we're talking about natural  forest regeneration  how is that different from actively  planted forests  well in this study which is the first of  its kind conducted by  trillian trees we tried to distinguish  between  uh plantation forests and natural  forests which either grow  grown spontaneously all by themselves or  perhaps have had some assistance through  enrichment planting or otherwise and  what we've  by doing that we're able to talk about  the success  of efforts to  restore natural forests which provide  greater carbon benefits greater  biodiversity benefits  and actually achieve that unplanted  forests and of course they stole their  carbon in the long term  whereas plantations you have a  harvesting cycle that that you  harvest the wood and then uses them in  products if short-lived that carbon  then returns to the atmosphere will  baldwin cantelo thanks very much indeed  coming up on tomorrow's daily climate  show i'll be speaking to the climate  activist louisa neubauer who's one of  the main organizers  of the fridays for future school strike  in germany  you can catch that here on sky news at  the same time and on our social channels  and website  thanks for watching see you tomorrow  you

